{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"aff1e6619618e8e28","tool_use_id":"toolu_01XDVVU6DE7mVdLyxj1wMeL4","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":76498,"tool_uses":19,"duration_ms":139869},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"30631430-bf0b-4089-826e-eb5778134701","session_id":"57df35a8-7776-42f2-9a89-3def6408fbf8"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01BSzaENhKF9Z6KnXtEiR8nm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RrhRkoxtk8NSqqmXuq3RkB","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"recursive_gen_depth_est","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260514T140607","overall_score":"borderline accept","report":"## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThis paper formalizes \"ordinal generation depth estimation\" — predicting how many recursive LLM generation cycles a text has undergone — constructs a benchmark (3 models x 3 strategies x 6 depths), proposes DCOL (a contrastive ordinal learning method with learnable margins), and provides a Depth Degradation Atlas characterizing feature evolution across depths. The task formalization and Atlas are genuinely interesting contributions, but the methodological novelty is incremental (CLOC + three engineering modifications), the related work coverage has notable gaps, and several claims are overstated relative to the evidence.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Genuine task novelty.** Ordinal depth estimation is a meaningful new problem formulation that goes beyond binary detection. The observation that the problem has non-uniform ordinal geometry (d0→d1 gap >> d3→d4 gap) is a real insight that justifies specialized methods beyond naive multi-class classification. This is not merely \"binary detection + ordinal regression\" — the non-uniform structure and the connection to model collapse dynamics give it scientific substance.\n\n2. **Atlas as an independent scientific contribution.** The systematic characterization of 19 features across 9 model-strategy combinations, the identification of var_surprisal as a near-universal depth indicator (monotonicity 0.961), the documentation of non-uniform inter-depth geometry, and the EDD metric collectively constitute a useful empirical contribution that would retain value even without the DCOL method. The finding that burstiness and type-token ratio exhibit non-monotonic behavior, connecting to Drayson et al.'s observations, adds depth.\n\n3. **Well-designed evaluation protocol.** The leave-one-model-out (60.1%) and leave-one-strategy-out (67.1%) experiments directly address the most important practical question (generalization to unseen generators). The cross-scale transfer to 8B and the wild-data calibration experiment provide ecological validity. The ablation is clean and each component's contribution is clearly isolated.\n\n4. **Coherent narrative arc.** The paper has an elegant \"observation → design → validation\" structure: Atlas documents non-uniform geometry → learnable margins are motivated by this geometry → learned margin ratios correlate with empirical distances (r > 0.95). This is good scientific storytelling.\n\n5. **Practical relevance well-articulated.** The graduated curation proof-of-concept, while small-scale, makes a convincing conceptual point: depth-aware weighting retains 20.7% more data at 7.1% lower PPL than binary filtering. The insight that the benefit comes from the graduated paradigm itself (not the specific weighting function) is well-supported by the sensitivity analysis.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **(Major) Methodological novelty is incremental.** DCOL is CLOC (Lu et al. 2025, CVPR) with three modifications: (a) learnable non-uniform margins replace fixed margins, (b) surprisal statistics are concatenated with CLS, (c) strategy embeddings modulate margins. Each modification is a straightforward engineering adaptation, not a conceptual advance. Learnable margins are a standard idea in metric learning (e.g., Wu et al. 2017 \"Sampling Matters in Deep Embedding Learning\"; Roth et al. 2020). Concatenating hand-crafted features with neural representations is standard practice. Strategy conditioning is a simple conditional architecture. The ablation shows only 2-4pp per component — meaningful but not transformative. The paper should more honestly position DCOL as \"CLOC adapted for text\" rather than implying it is a novel framework.\n\n2. **(Major) Related work coverage has significant gaps.** Several important areas are insufficiently discussed:\n   - **Watermarking-based provenance:** Kirchenbauer et al. (2023, \"A Watermark for Large Language Models\") and subsequent work (Zhao et al. 2023, Christ et al. 2024) are entirely missing. Watermarking is the most direct competitor for tracking text provenance and generation history. The paper should discuss why statistical depth estimation is needed when watermarking exists, and acknowledge that watermarking could in principle encode depth information.\n   - **Data quality filtering and curation:** The graduated curation claim (Contribution 3) is made without citing any of the substantial literature on data quality scoring: DSIR (Xie et al. 2023), QuRating (Wettig et al. 2024), DataComp (Gadre et al. 2024), or the perplexity-based filtering in CCNet/RedPajama. These methods already perform graduated quality filtering — the paper should explain what depth-based curation adds beyond existing quality proxies.\n   - **Multi-class synthetic text detection:** The paper claims to be the first to go beyond binary, but there is prior work on distinguishing between multiple generators (e.g., Uchendu et al. 2020 \"Authorship Attribution for Neural Text Generation\"; Li et al. 2024 on multi-source attribution). While these don't estimate *recursive depth*, they do perform multi-class classification of synthetic text origins, and the distinction should be drawn clearly.\n   - **Data provenance and attribution:** The broader literature on data attribution (Hammoudeh & Lowd 2024), dataset cartography (Swayamdipta et al. 2020), and training data influence is relevant but uncited.\n\n3. **(Major) The \"fundamentally different from binary detection\" claim is overstated.** The paper argues depth estimation is \"fundamentally different\" from binary detection, but the evidence undercuts this: (a) the features used (surprisal variance, PPL statistics) are the *same* features that power binary detectors; (b) binary detection accuracy is 96.1% — the task is essentially solved at the binary level; (c) the real contribution is the *ordinal extension*, not a fundamentally different signal. A more defensible claim would be \"ordinal depth estimation is a meaningful generalization of binary detection that requires ordinal-aware methods.\" The current framing overpromises.\n\n4. **(Minor) \"First systematic empirical characterization\" claim needs qualification.** Drayson et al. (2025) already characterize multi-generational degradation empirically across Pythia models, including documenting non-monotonic phenomena. The Atlas extends this with more features (19 vs. fewer), more models (3 vs. 1), and a structured framework (EDD), but it is not the \"first\" characterization — it is the most comprehensive. The paper should cite Drayson et al. more carefully and position the Atlas as extending their work systematically.\n\n5. **(Minor) Ecological validity of depth-5 recursive text.** The paper's motivation relies on encountering deeply recursive text in the wild, but the wild-data calibration shows that depth 4+ content comprises only 2.4% of predictions. The practical scenario of multiple rounds of model→data→model→data→... feedback loops producing depth-5 text at web scale is speculative. In realistic settings, most \"contamination\" is depth-1 (direct generation). The paper should more honestly scope the contribution: depth estimation at depths 0-2 has clear practical value; depths 3-5 are more of scientific interest for understanding model collapse dynamics.\n\n6. **(Minor) Benchmark uses only small models (1-1.5B, one 8B probe).** All three benchmark models are in the 1-1.5B range. The 8B transfer experiment is 3-class only (depths 0-2). Given that contemporary LLMs are 7B-70B+, and degradation rates scale inversely with capacity (as the paper itself notes — 8B degrades at 0.67x the rate), the benchmark's relevance to production-scale models is uncertain. The cross-scale transfer gap of 13.3pp is substantial.\n\n7. **(Minor) The contrastive loss formulation (Eq. 1) appears incomplete.** The loss as written only penalizes when embedding distance *exceeds* the margin, but there is no term pushing apart embeddings of different depths when they are *too close*. Standard contrastive ordinal losses include both an attractive and repulsive term. Is this a simplification for presentation, or the actual loss? If the latter, it should be discussed why a one-sided loss suffices.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. How does DCOL perform when the recursive chain involves mixed decoding strategies (e.g., depth 1 uses nucleus but depth 2 uses temperature)? Real-world recursive loops would not maintain a consistent decoding strategy.\n\n2. The surprisal-augmented anchors require computing token-level surprisal under a \"reference model.\" Which model is used? If it is one of the benchmark models (e.g., Pythia-1.4B), doesn't this create a circularity where the detector requires access to a model from the same family it is trying to detect?\n\n3. What is the computational overhead of DCOL at inference time compared to standard DeBERTa classification? The surprisal computation requires a forward pass through the reference model, effectively doubling inference cost.\n\n4. The learned margin ratios (1.00, 0.68, 0.52, 0.41, 0.35) correlate with empirical inter-depth distances (r > 0.95). But the margins are learned on the same data from which the empirical distances are computed. This is circular validation. Would the margins learned on, say, Pythia-only data still correlate with OLMo's inter-depth distances?\n\n5. For the graduated curation experiment: is the \"binary filtering\" baseline using an oracle (ground-truth labels) or a trained binary detector? If oracle, the comparison is unfair to binary filtering since graduated curation uses predicted (noisy) depths while binary uses perfect labels.\n\n6. The EDD threshold of AUC >= 0.75 is arbitrary. How sensitive are the EDD values to this threshold? At 0.80, does EDD drop substantially?\n\n### Missing References or Related Work\n\n- **Kirchenbauer et al. (2023)** \"A Watermark for Large Language Models\" — watermarking as an alternative/complementary approach to generation tracking\n- **Xie et al. (2023)** \"Data Selection for Language Models via Importance Resampling\" (DSIR) — graduated data quality filtering\n- **Wettig et al. (2024)** \"QuRating: Selecting High-Quality Data for Training Language Models\" — learned quality scores for data curation\n- **Uchendu et al. (2020)** \"Authorship Attribution for Neural Text Generation\" — multi-class classification of synthetic text (by generator, not depth, but relevant precedent)\n- **Wu et al. (2017)** \"Sampling Matters in Deep Embedding Learning\" — learnable margins in metric learning (prior art for the \"learnable margins\" modification)\n- **Roth et al. (2020)** \"Revisiting Training Strategies and Generalization Performance in Deep Metric Learning\" — adaptive margins\n- **Swayamdipta et al. (2020)** \"Dataset Cartography\" — data quality characterization relevant to the Atlas framing\n- **Guo et al. (2024)** or related work on multi-generational synthetic data detection beyond binary — the paper should conduct a more thorough search for any concurrent work on multi-class depth/generation estimation\n- **Broader data provenance literature** — the framing as \"depth estimation\" could be connected to data lineage tracking in database/systems literature\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: 4/5\n- Excitement: 3/5\n- Overall Assessment: 3/5\n\n### Confidence\n4/5 — I am confident in my assessment of the novelty and related work aspects. I have strong familiarity with the machine-generated text detection, contrastive/metric learning, and model collapse literatures. I am less certain about whether there is concurrent unpublished work on multi-class generation depth estimation.\n\n### Detailed Justification\n\nThis paper identifies a genuinely interesting problem — ordinal depth estimation of recursively generated text — and provides a competent, thorough investigation. The Atlas and EDD metric are the most novel contributions: they advance our empirical understanding of model collapse dynamics in a structured way that will be useful to the community regardless of the specific detection method used. The task formalization itself has value, as it reframes the binary detection problem in a way that enables more nuanced data curation decisions.\n\nHowever, the methodological contribution (DCOL) is incremental. It is CLOC with three sensible but individually standard engineering modifications. The 10.1pp improvement over CORAL-DeBERTa is convincing, but this is the expected outcome when you (a) add task-relevant features (surprisal), (b) match the loss geometry to the data geometry (learnable margins), and (c) condition on known metadata (strategy). None of these ideas are new in the metric/contrastive learning literature. The paper would be stronger if it positioned DCOL as \"the right instantiation of contrastive ordinal learning for this task\" rather than implying architectural novelty.\n\nThe related work gaps are concerning for an EMNLP submission. The absence of watermarking literature, data quality/curation methods (DSIR, QuRating), and multi-class synthetic text attribution weakens the positioning. The \"fundamentally different from binary detection\" framing is overstated — the features and signals are shared; what is different is the granularity of the prediction target and the need for ordinal-aware methods. A more measured framing would strengthen the paper.\n\nOverall, this is a solid 3: an acceptable paper with a genuinely new task formulation and useful empirical contributions, but incremental methodology and incomplete related work coverage. Addressing the related work gaps, toning down the \"fundamentally different\" claim, and more honestly positioning the methodological contribution would move this toward a 4. The Atlas alone is a meaningful contribution to the model collapse literature, and the graduated curation proof-of-concept, while small, opens an interesting direction.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":714,"cache_read_input_tokens":75290,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":714,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XDVVU6DE7mVdLyxj1wMeL4","session_id":"57df35a8-7776-42f2-9a89-3def6408fbf8","uuid":"8bb89d2d-ac98-4110-ab5b-5c1a91dbf1e8"}
