{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011JcaiF231y6TXEWVzq7Lpm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I'll synthesize the roundtable and submit the consolidated report.\n\n**Roundtable Summary:**\n\n- **R1** (Methods+Experiments): Soundness 3, Excitement 4, Overall 3. Key concerns: loss formulation missing repulsive term, surprisal anchor circularity, d0 confound inflating accuracy, k-NN underspecified.\n- **R2** (Novelty+Related Work): Soundness 4, Excitement 3, Overall 3. Key concerns: incremental methodology (CLOC + standard modifications), significant related work gaps, overstated \"fundamentally different\" claim.\n\n**Convergence:** Both agree on Overall 3 (borderline accept). Both value the task formulation and Atlas. They diverge on where the weakness lies — R1 sees methodological gaps, R2 sees novelty/positioning gaps. These are complementary, not contradictory."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1512,"cache_read_input_tokens":80813,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1512},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"57df35a8-7776-42f2-9a89-3def6408fbf8","uuid":"be2a334c-c350-4ed6-9e70-e94c4cdfa5a0"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011JcaiF231y6TXEWVzq7Lpm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011zumCbfpzPD6o6BXQXK9mm","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"\n# Dream Review Report: Ordinal Depth Estimation of Recursive LLM Generations via Contrastive Learning with Learnable Margins\n\n**Venue:** EMNLP 2026 (ACL Rolling Review)\n**Review Mode:** Full (2 Reviewers + Roundtable)\n**Date:** 2026-05-14\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer Scores Summary\n\n| Dimension | R1 (Methods+Experiments) | R2 (Novelty+Related Work) | Consensus |\n|-----------|--------------------------|---------------------------|-----------|\n| Soundness | 3/5 | 4/5 | 3.5/5 |\n| Excitement | 4/5 | 3/5 | 3.5/5 |\n| Overall Assessment | 3/5 | 3/5 | **3/5 (Borderline Accept)** |\n| Confidence | 4/5 | 4/5 | — |\n\n---\n\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n\nThe paper formalizes ordinal generation depth estimation and proposes DCOL with three text-specific modifications atop a DeBERTa encoder. Experimental coverage is impressive (14 experiments spanning benchmark construction, generalization, ablation, and downstream application), and the Atlas provides a useful empirical characterization. However, several methodological concerns — the loss formulation, surprisal circularity, benchmark confounds, and underspecified inference — prevent full endorsement.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Comprehensive experimental design.** All 14 planned experiments are covered. The combination of leave-one-model-out (Table 8), leave-one-strategy-out (Table 9), cross-scale transfer (Table 10), ablation (Table 11), granularity analysis (Table 12), wild-data calibration (Table 13), and downstream application (Table 14) addresses most natural reviewer questions preemptively.\n\n2. **Well-motivated method design.** The observation-to-design-to-validation loop is clean: the Atlas documents non-uniform inter-depth geometry (d01/d34 ratio = 2.83), this motivates learnable non-uniform margins, and the learned margin ratios (1.00, 0.68, 0.52, 0.41, 0.35) correlate strongly (r > 0.95) with the empirical distances.\n\n3. **Strong baselines and fair comparisons.** Seven baselines spanning binary detector repurposing, ordinal regression, standard classification, direct regression, and zero-shot prompting. The inclusion of CORAL-DeBERTa (same encoder) isolates DCOL's modifications from encoder choice.\n\n4. **Ablation is convincing.** Each component contributes 2–4pp independently; sum of individual drops (9.2pp) slightly exceeds joint drop (8.1pp) indicating mild positive interaction; \"w/o all three\" (64.1%) still outperforms CORAL (62.1%) by 2.0pp, cleanly separating framework from modification contributions.\n\n5. **Atlas as independent scientific contribution.** The EDD metric, monotonicity analysis, and non-uniform geometry documentation have value beyond DCOL. var_surprisal's 0.961 monotonicity is a genuinely useful result for the model collapse community.\n\n6. **Statistical significance reported** for DCOL vs. CORAL (paired bootstrap, p < 0.001) with confidence intervals for key metrics.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **(Major) Loss formulation (Eq. 1) appears incomplete.** The loss L = Σ max(0, ||f(xi) - f(xj)|| - m(δij))² only penalizes when embedding distance *exceeds* the margin — a ceiling/pull term. There is no repulsive mechanism pushing apart samples at different depths. As written, the loss is trivially minimized by collapsing all embeddings to a single point. Either this is a presentation error (missing the repulsive term) or additional loss terms exist but are not described. This is a critical reproducibility concern.\n\n2. **(Major) Circularity in surprisal-augmented anchors.** The surprisal features appear to be computed using Pythia-1.4B as reference model. For Pythia-generated text, this creates a tautological signal — the generating model's own surprisal on its own output conflates model identity with depth. The paper does not specify: (a) which reference model is used across all three families, (b) whether the same reference is used for all text, (c) how this affects cross-model generalization. The surprisal anchors contribute 3.1pp in ablation, so this is not ignorable.\n\n3. **(Major) Depth-0 confound.** Depth-0 is Wikipedia (human); depths 1–5 are model-generated. The d0 vs. d1 AUC of 0.998 is essentially binary human-vs-machine detection, not depth estimation. Per-class accuracy (d0: 91.2% vs. d1: 79.3%) and binary detection (96.1%) confirm the d0 signal is dominated by the human-vs-synthetic distinction. The 72.2% headline is substantially inflated by near-perfect d0 classification. A 5-class (d1–d5) accuracy should be reported alongside.\n\n4. **(Major) k-NN inference underspecified.** No k value, distance metric, or sensitivity analysis provided. If results are brittle to k, the method has a hidden hyperparameter undermining reported numbers.\n\n5. **(Minor) Cross-scale evaluation is limited.** The 8B probe uses only 3-class (depth 0–2), making it incomparable to 6-class main results. Only one larger model tested, only to depth 2. The claim \"depth signals transfer across model scales\" is stronger than this evidence supports.\n\n6. **(Minor) Downstream curation lacks task-based evaluation.** PPL on held-out RedPajama subset could reflect distributional matching rather than genuine quality improvement. No evaluation on downstream tasks (MMLU, reasoning).\n\n7. **(Minor) No error analysis.** No discussion of what text properties make depth estimation harder, no confusion matrix shown (only described).\n\n8. **(Minor) Wild-data calibration has no ground truth.** Correlations with PPL/AlignScore are suggestive but could mean DCOL is learning a perplexity proxy. Partial correlation (-0.198) mitigates this partially.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. Provide the complete training objective including the repulsive/floor term.\n2. Which reference model computes surprisal? Is it the same for all three model families?\n3. What k is used in k-NN? What is the sensitivity?\n4. What is the 5-class (d1–d5 only) accuracy?\n5. How is the margin monotonicity constraint implemented (projection? reparameterization?)?\n6. How is strategy conditioning handled at inference on wild data where strategy is unknown?\n7. Has depth-0 been tested with non-Wikipedia seed corpora?\n8. Can you provide error bars for the non-monotonic d4v5 AUC in Table 4?\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: 3/5\n- Excitement: 4/5\n- Overall Assessment: 3/5\n- Confidence: 4/5\n\n---\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n\nThe paper introduces a well-defined new task (ordinal depth estimation) and provides the first systematic characterization of recursive degradation dynamics (Atlas). However, the DCOL method is an incremental adaptation of CLOC (CVPR 2025) with three standard engineering modifications, the related work has significant gaps, and certain claims are overstated relative to the evidence.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Genuine task novelty.** Ordinal depth estimation is a legitimately new problem formulation. The insight that binary detection discards actionable ordinal information is simple but important. The task definition is clean and the benchmark construction is well-motivated.\n\n2. **Atlas is a meaningful scientific contribution.** The systematic characterization of 19 features across 3 models × 3 strategies × 6 depths, the EDD metric, and the documentation of non-uniform inter-depth geometry go beyond what Drayson et al. (2025) provide. Drayson et al. characterize degradation in Pythia only; this paper extends to three architectures and formalizes the findings.\n\n3. **The graduated curation paradigm is a useful conceptual contribution.** Even as a proof-of-concept, the insight that depth-aware weighting is a Pareto improvement over binary filtering (more data retained, lower PPL) is valuable for the data curation community.\n\n4. **Comprehensive evaluation.** The paper addresses cross-model, cross-strategy, and cross-scale generalization — a thorough treatment of the external validity question.\n\n5. **Elegant granularity analysis.** The finding that above-chance margin *increases* with finer granularity (Table 12) is a genuinely surprising and well-presented result that justifies the ordinal framing.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **(Major) Incremental methodology.** DCOL is CLOC + three off-the-shelf modifications:\n   - Learnable margins are standard in metric learning (Wu et al. 2017, Roth et al. 2020).\n   - Surprisal-augmented anchors are feature concatenation before a projection head — a standard technique.\n   - Strategy-aware conditioning is a lightweight FiLM-style modulation.\n   Each contributes only 2–4pp. The paper should position DCOL as \"CLOC adapted for text depth estimation\" rather than a novel framework. The novelty lies in the task and the Atlas, not the method.\n\n2. **(Major) Significant related work gaps.**\n   - **Watermarking:** Kirchenbauer et al. (2023) is entirely absent — watermarking-based provenance tracking is a direct alternative to post-hoc depth estimation.\n   - **Data quality / curation:** DSIR (Xie et al. 2023), QuRating (Wettig et al. 2024) — the graduated curation contribution should be positioned against the data selection literature.\n   - **Multi-class synthetic attribution:** Uchendu et al. (2020) and subsequent work on authorship attribution across multiple generators — a precursor to multi-class synthetic text analysis.\n   - **Data provenance:** The broader data attribution and provenance literature (e.g., data Shapley, influence functions) is relevant to tracking generation history.\n   - **Model collapse theory:** Alemohammad et al. (2023) \"Self-Consuming Generative Models Go MAD\" and Briesch et al. (2023) are missing.\n   - **Binary detection:** Fast-DetectGPT (Bao et al. 2024) should be included in the baseline battery.\n\n3. **(Major) Overstated \"fundamentally different\" claim.** The paper claims ordinal depth estimation is \"fundamentally different from binary detection\" (Section 1). But the features powering depth estimation (surprisal variance, perplexity statistics) are the *same* features used by binary detectors. The contribution is an ordinal *generalization* of binary detection — important, but not \"fundamentally different.\" A more honest framing: \"binary detection is a special case; ordinal estimation extracts strictly more information from the same signal space.\"\n\n4. **(Minor) Circularity in margin-geometry validation.** Reporting Pearson r > 0.95 between learned margins and empirical inter-depth distances is circular: the margins were *trained* on the same data from which the Atlas distances were computed. The margins *should* correlate — this validates convergence, not the design principle. A genuine validation would train margins on one model family and show they match the Atlas geometry of a held-out family.\n\n5. **(Minor) EDD threshold sensitivity.** EDD is defined with a fixed AUC ≥ 0.75 threshold. No sensitivity analysis is provided. If the threshold were 0.80, EDD values would change substantially (several entries in Table 2 would drop by 1). The paper should discuss threshold choice or provide a range.\n\n6. **(Minor) Graduated vs. binary comparison fairness.** Binary filtering retains 71.6% of data; graduated retains 92.3%. The PPL improvement could partly reflect having 20% more training data, not the weighting scheme. A fair comparison would match data volume — e.g., binary filtering with a higher threshold retaining ~92% of data.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. How does DCOL differ from CLOC beyond the three modifications? Is the base contrastive framework identical?\n2. Can the margin-geometry correlation be validated on a held-out model family not used in training?\n3. What happens to EDD if the threshold is changed to 0.70 or 0.80?\n4. Is the reference model for surprisal features the same model that generated the text? How does this affect the claim that depth signals are \"model-agnostic\"?\n5. Has the graduated curation been compared against volume-matched binary filtering (same data retention rate)?\n6. How does DCOL handle text generated by mixed or unknown strategies in deployment?\n\n### Missing References\n\n- Kirchenbauer et al. (2023) — watermarking for LLM outputs\n- Xie et al. (2023) — DSIR data selection\n- Wettig et al. (2024) — QuRating data quality\n- Uchendu et al. (2020) — multi-class authorship attribution for generators\n- Alemohammad et al. (2023) — self-consuming generative models\n- Briesch et al. (2023) — LLMs suffering from own output\n- Bao et al. (2024) — Fast-DetectGPT\n- Guo et al. (2024) — linguistic diversity decline in recursive generations\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: 4/5\n- Excitement: 3/5\n- Overall Assessment: 3/5\n- Confidence: 4/5\n\n---\n\n## Roundtable Discussion\n\n### Step 2: Cross-Sharing\n\nR1 and R2 exchanged reviews. Key observations from each reviewer on the other's points:\n\n**R1 on R2's concerns:**\n- Agrees that DCOL's novelty is incremental — the observation-to-design loop is clean but the components themselves are standard. The paper's main novelty is the task + Atlas, not DCOL per se.\n- Agrees the related work gaps are significant, especially watermarking (Kirchenbauer et al.) and data curation literature (DSIR, QuRating).\n- Notes the \"fundamentally different\" framing is indeed overstated — \"ordinal generalization\" is more accurate.\n\n**R2 on R1's concerns:**\n- Agrees the loss formulation issue (Eq. 1 missing repulsive term) is the single most critical technical concern. If this is a presentation error, it's fixable; if it's a genuine design flaw, it undermines the entire method.\n- Finds the d0 confound argument compelling — the 6-class headline number is inflated. A 5-class (d1–d5) metric should be foregrounded.\n- The surprisal circularity is concerning but considers it partially mitigated by the cross-model LOO results (60.1%) where the reference model is presumably not the generating model for the held-out family.\n\n### Step 3: Convergence\n\n**Points of agreement:**\n1. The **task formulation** (ordinal depth estimation) is genuinely novel and timely — both reviewers value this.\n2. The **Atlas** is an independent scientific contribution with value beyond DCOL.\n3. The **loss formulation** in Eq. 1 is either incomplete or incorrectly presented — this is the top priority for revision.\n4. The **related work** has significant gaps that need addressing (watermarking, data curation, multi-class attribution).\n5. The **6-class headline** is inflated by the d0 confound — a d1–d5 metric should be reported.\n6. The **DCOL method** is technically competent but incrementally novel — the paper should reframe DCOL as an adaptation of CLOC, not a novel framework.\n7. The **experimental coverage** is a clear strength — 14 experiments addressing diverse validity questions.\n8. **Overall assessment: 3/5** (borderline accept) — the task and Atlas are strong enough to carry the paper, but methodological and positioning issues need attention.\n\n**Points of partial disagreement:**\n- R1 rates Excitement 4/5 (problem is highly novel and timely), R2 rates 3/5 (execution is incremental). Convergence: **3.5/5** — the problem is exciting but the methodological contribution is moderate.\n- R1 rates Soundness 3/5 (loss formulation, circularity), R2 rates 4/5 (experimental design is thorough). Convergence: **3.5/5** — sound experimental design with a critical presentation gap in the loss function.\n\n### Step 4: Consolidated Assessment\n\n**Overall Score: 3/5 — Borderline Accept**\n\n**Verdict:** This paper makes a genuine contribution by formalizing a new task (ordinal depth estimation) and providing the first cross-model empirical characterization of recursive degradation dynamics. The experimental coverage is exemplary. However, the paper has three categories of issues that prevent a strong accept:\n\n**(1) Critical technical gap:** The loss function (Eq. 1) as presented is incomplete — it lacks a repulsive term and would be trivially minimized by embedding collapse. This must be clarified for reproducibility.\n\n**(2) Positioning and framing issues:** DCOL is an incremental adaptation of CLOC, not a novel framework. The \"fundamentally different from binary detection\" claim is overstated. The 6-class headline is inflated by the near-trivial d0 classification. The related work has notable gaps (watermarking, data curation, multi-class attribution).\n\n**(3) Methodological concerns:** Surprisal anchor circularity, k-NN inference underspecification, limited cross-scale evaluation, and the downstream curation experiment lacking task-based evaluation.\n\n**If the authors address (1) and (2) in revision — clarifying the complete loss, repositioning DCOL as adapted-CLOC, reporting d1–d5 accuracy, and filling related work gaps — the paper would likely reach a solid accept.** The task formalization and Atlas alone justify publication at a top venue if the method section meets basic reproducibility standards.\n\n**Recommendation for authors (priority-ordered):**\n1. Provide the complete training loss (including repulsive/floor term)\n2. Report 5-class (d1–d5) accuracy as a secondary headline metric\n3. Clarify the reference model used for surprisal computation\n4. Specify k-NN hyperparameters and sensitivity\n5. Add missing citations (Kirchenbauer, DSIR, Alemohammad, Fast-DetectGPT, Uchendu)\n6. Reframe DCOL as \"CLOC adapted for text depth estimation\" — the novelty is the task + Atlas\n7. Add EDD threshold sensitivity analysis\n8. Discuss graduated vs. binary curation with matched data volume\n\n---\n\n## Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\nAll 14 imagined experiments are represented in the paper:\n\n| # | Experiment | Status | Coverage Quality |\n|---|-----------|--------|-----------------|\n| 1 | OLMo-1B Recursive Chain | ✓ | Table 3 — full results |\n| 2 | GPT-2 XL Recursive Chain | ✓ | Table 3 — full results |\n| 3 | DCOL Training | ✓ | Tables 5, 6 — full results |\n| 4 | Leave-One-Model-Out | ✓ | Table 8 — full results |\n| 5 | Depth Degradation Atlas | ✓ | Tables 1, 2, §3.4 — comprehensive |\n| 6 | Leave-One-Strategy-Out | ✓ | Table 9 — full results |\n| 7 | DeBERTa E2E | ✓ | Table 7 — full results |\n| 8 | Baseline Battery | ✓ | Table 5 — 7 baselines |\n| 9 | DCOL Ablation | ✓ | Table 11 — full results |\n| 10 | Depth Extension | ✓ | Table 4, §5.2 — full results |\n| 11 | Granularity Ablation | ✓ | Table 12 — full results |\n| 12 | Graduated Curation | ✓ | Table 14 — full results |\n| 13 | 7B Cross-Scale Probe | ✓ | Table 10 — reduced scope (3-class) |\n| 14 | Wild-Data Calibration | ✓ | Table 13 — full results |\n\n**Coverage: 14/14 experiments present.** The cross-scale probe (#13) has reduced scope (3-class instead of 6-class, depth 0–2 only), which is noted as a minor weakness. All other experiments match or exceed the imagined design's scope.\n\n**Notable:** The paper includes no figures (only tables), which is unusual for a 10-page paper at EMNLP. The imagined experiment design describes several key visualizations (confusion matrix heatmap, Atlas heatmap grid, AUC decay curves, feature importance comparison, t-SNE, waterfall chart) that are entirely absent from the paper. This is a significant presentation gap — these visualizations would substantially improve readability and impact.\n","project":"recursive_gen_depth_est","tier":"best","dream_id":"20260514T140607","overall_score":"borderline accept","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1512,"cache_read_input_tokens":80813,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1512},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"57df35a8-7776-42f2-9a89-3def6408fbf8","uuid":"2cf24d70-4e57-4ec8-80d2-1815f431efc2"}
